1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,011
A dam holds water high behind a wall.

2
00:00:03,011 --> 00:00:06,188
Let it through a gap,
and it spins a turbine.

3
00:00:06,188 --> 00:00:10,030
Let air out of a balloon, and
it spins a paper pinwheel.

4
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:12,342
Pile something up on one side.

5
00:00:12,342 --> 00:00:14,726
When it rushes back, it can do work.

6
00:00:15,176 --> 00:00:17,241
Aarav saw a dam on a school trip.

7
00:00:17,241 --> 00:00:19,932
He asked: does a leaf
have a dam inside it?

8
00:00:19,932 --> 00:00:23,203
And how does it make ATP,
the energy coin of the cell?

9
00:00:23,553 --> 00:00:25,179
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:25,179 --> 00:00:26,079
Yes.

11
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:28,423
The leaf piles up protons
inside the thylakoid.

12
00:00:28,423 --> 00:00:31,730
As they rush out through a
tiny machine, ATP is made.

13
00:00:32,230 --> 00:00:35,789
By the end, you will know photophosphorylation,
cyclic and non cyclic flow,

14
00:00:35,789 --> 00:00:37,332
and the chemiosmotic hypothesis.

15
00:00:37,982 --> 00:00:38,923
First, a long word.

16
00:00:38,923 --> 00:00:39,823
Photophosphorylation.

17
00:00:40,103 --> 00:00:42,699
ATP is made by adding a phosphate to ADP.

18
00:00:42,699 --> 00:00:45,314
Adding a phosphate is
called phosphorylation.

19
00:00:45,764 --> 00:00:49,160
When this happens using the energy
of light, it is photophosphorylation.

20
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:50,140
Photo means light.

21
00:00:50,790 --> 00:00:52,861
Let's zoom in to where ATP is made.

22
00:00:53,211 --> 00:00:56,446
Inside a chloroplast, the
thylakoids are flat sacs.

23
00:00:56,446 --> 00:00:59,155
The space inside a thylakoid is the lumen.

24
00:00:59,155 --> 00:01:00,677
Outside is the stroma.

25
00:01:01,177 --> 00:01:03,710
Zoom closer to the thylakoid membrane.

26
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:07,080
This thin wall separates
the lumen from the stroma.

27
00:01:07,580 --> 00:01:09,230
Zoom in on the membrane.

28
00:01:09,230 --> 00:01:11,513
In it sits the enzyme ATP synthase.

29
00:01:11,513 --> 00:01:14,861
Its part CF zero forms a
channel through the membrane.

30
00:01:14,861 --> 00:01:17,633
Its part CF one sticks
out into the stroma.

31
00:01:18,283 --> 00:01:19,989
Now watch chemiosmosis, step by step.

32
00:01:20,339 --> 00:01:21,239
Step one.

33
00:01:20,999 --> 00:01:23,526
Water splits on the inner
side of the membrane.

34
00:01:23,526 --> 00:01:25,224
Its protons stay in the lumen.

35
00:01:25,974 --> 00:01:26,874
Step two.

36
00:01:26,619 --> 00:01:29,960
As electrons move between the
photosystems, protons are carried across

37
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:32,381
the membrane, from the
stroma into the lumen.

38
00:01:33,131 --> 00:01:34,031
Step three.

39
00:01:33,867 --> 00:01:37,572
NADP reductase, on the stroma side,
uses protons from the stroma to make

40
00:01:37,572 --> 00:01:38,472
NADPH.

41
00:01:38,810 --> 00:01:39,710
Step four.

42
00:01:39,549 --> 00:01:43,184
Now there are many protons in
the lumen, and few in the stroma.

43
00:01:43,184 --> 00:01:45,274
This difference is a proton gradient.

44
00:01:45,274 --> 00:01:47,015
The lumen becomes more acidic.

45
00:01:47,765 --> 00:01:48,665
Step five.

46
00:01:48,442 --> 00:01:50,926
Protons rush back to the
stroma through CF zero.

47
00:01:50,926 --> 00:01:53,978
The energy changes the shape
of CF one, and it makes ATP.

48
00:01:54,728 --> 00:01:55,628
Remember this.

49
00:01:55,455 --> 00:01:56,855
Protons pile up in the lumen.

50
00:01:57,455 --> 00:01:58,963
So chemiosmosis needs four things.

51
00:01:59,313 --> 00:02:00,213
A membrane.

52
00:02:00,102 --> 00:02:02,137
A proton pump to move the protons.

53
00:02:02,137 --> 00:02:04,292
A proton gradient across the membrane.

54
00:02:04,742 --> 00:02:07,958
And ATP synthase, the machine
that turns the flow into ATP.

55
00:02:07,958 --> 00:02:11,807
The same idea works in mitochondria,
but there protons pile up in the space

56
00:02:11,807 --> 00:02:13,261
between its two membranes.

57
00:02:13,911 --> 00:02:16,002
There are two ways electrons can flow.

58
00:02:16,002 --> 00:02:17,085
Let's compare them.

59
00:02:17,435 --> 00:02:18,335
Non cyclic.

60
00:02:18,271 --> 00:02:22,518
Photosystem two and photosystem one
work in series, one after the other.

61
00:02:22,518 --> 00:02:24,909
Electrons go all the way to NADP plus.

62
00:02:24,909 --> 00:02:26,737
Both ATP and NADPH are made.

63
00:02:27,187 --> 00:02:28,087
Cyclic.

64
00:02:27,825 --> 00:02:29,602
Only photosystem one works.

65
00:02:29,602 --> 00:02:33,224
The electron goes round and
comes back to photosystem one.

66
00:02:33,224 --> 00:02:35,213
Only ATP is made, no NADPH.

67
00:02:35,863 --> 00:02:37,643
Where does cyclic flow happen?

68
00:02:37,993 --> 00:02:39,458
In the stroma lamellae.

69
00:02:39,458 --> 00:02:42,989
Their membranes have no photosystem
two and no NADP reductase.

70
00:02:43,439 --> 00:02:47,342
It also happens when only light of
wavelength beyond six hundred eighty

71
00:02:47,342 --> 00:02:48,815
nanometres is available.

72
00:02:49,415 --> 00:02:50,315
Your turn.

73
00:02:50,031 --> 00:02:51,979
Does cyclic
photophosphorylation make NADPH?

74
00:02:51,979 --> 00:02:52,879
Yes or no?

75
00:02:52,638 --> 00:02:53,545
Pause and decide.

76
00:02:56,745 --> 00:02:57,645
No!

77
00:02:57,077 --> 00:02:58,448
Cyclic flow makes only ATP.

78
00:02:59,098 --> 00:03:00,546
Now, a worked example.

79
00:03:00,546 --> 00:03:01,446
Four clues.

80
00:03:01,335 --> 00:03:02,784
Cyclic, or non cyclic?

81
00:03:03,134 --> 00:03:05,095
Clue one: oxygen is released.

82
00:03:05,095 --> 00:03:07,775
That needs water splitting, so non cyclic.

83
00:03:07,775 --> 00:03:10,165
Clue two: happens in stroma lamellae.

84
00:03:10,165 --> 00:03:11,065
Cyclic.

85
00:03:11,231 --> 00:03:13,372
Clue three: both ATP and NADPH are made.

86
00:03:13,372 --> 00:03:14,272
Non cyclic.

87
00:03:14,518 --> 00:03:17,946
Clue four: only light beyond
six hundred eighty nanometres.

88
00:03:17,946 --> 00:03:18,846
Cyclic.

89
00:03:19,185 --> 00:03:20,646
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:03:20,996 --> 00:03:22,997
Many students think protons
pile up in the stroma.

91
00:03:23,397 --> 00:03:25,103
But protons pile up in the lumen.

92
00:03:25,103 --> 00:03:28,566
They flow out to the stroma through
CF zero, and CF one makes ATP on the

93
00:03:28,566 --> 00:03:29,466
stroma side.

94
00:03:29,930 --> 00:03:31,226
Where is this asked?

95
00:03:31,226 --> 00:03:35,351
As statement and match questions on
CF zero, CF one, the lumen and cyclic

96
00:03:35,351 --> 00:03:36,251
flow.

97
00:03:36,438 --> 00:03:37,588
Exam level now.

98
00:03:37,588 --> 00:03:39,285
Read the four statements.

99
00:03:39,285 --> 00:03:40,484
Pause and solve.

100
00:03:45,884 --> 00:03:47,093
The answer is C.

101
00:03:47,093 --> 00:03:49,737
Statements one, two and four are correct.

102
00:03:50,237 --> 00:03:53,729
Water splits on the inner side,
and the lumen becomes more acidic.

103
00:03:53,729 --> 00:03:55,751
CF one makes ATP on the stroma side.

104
00:03:56,251 --> 00:03:58,135
But cyclic photophosphorylation
makes no NADPH.

105
00:03:58,135 --> 00:03:59,377
So statement three is wrong.

106
00:03:59,977 --> 00:04:01,517
Here is your recap.

107
00:04:01,517 --> 00:04:03,100
Screenshot this card.

108
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:08,486
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:04:08,886 --> 00:04:09,786
One.

110
00:04:09,129 --> 00:04:10,029
What is photophosphorylation?

111
00:04:10,924 --> 00:04:11,824
Two.

112
00:04:11,346 --> 00:04:13,813
How is cyclic flow
different from non cyclic?

113
00:04:14,713 --> 00:04:15,613
Three.

114
00:04:15,113 --> 00:04:16,825
Name the four things chemiosmosis needs.

115
00:04:17,925 --> 00:04:21,929
Next time: where does all this ATP and
NADPH go, and how is carbon dioxide

116
00:04:21,929 --> 00:04:23,027
turned into sugar?

117
00:04:23,027 --> 00:04:24,901
We find out in the Calvin cycle.
